 "productionNotes": "No source consulted for this page gives a production total for the US Vanagon, and none gives a count of US Westfalia campers or US Syncros. What can be stated is narrower. Heritage Parts Centre, a parts supplier, gives 1.3 million Type 25 (T3) vans built at Hannover between 1979 and 1992; that figure is for the Hannover plant across every market and body, it is single-sourced here, and it excludes other assembly points, so it is not a Vanagon number and is not used as the productionTotal. For the Syncro there are two figures. Live the Van Life attributes 45,478 T3 Syncros built through 1992 to Volkswagen; Silodrome gives 43,468 for a 1984 to 1992 production run. The gap is 2,010 vans and neither page shows its working, so the Syncro figure is carried as disputed. The two pages also disagree on the start: Silodrome and Hagerty's Rob Siegel put the Syncro at 1984, Live the Van Life says Volkswagen introduced the production Syncro in 1985, and Curbside Classic records it as new to the US for the 1986 model year. US sales are the closest thing to a US production record found. Curbside Classic gives 12,669 Vanagons sold in the US in 1986 and 5,416 in 1988, and says sales stayed in the 5,000 range through the end of production in 1991. Earlier years are not given in any source fetched, so no US total can be built from them. The westfaliat3.info model history lists the US Westfalia campers by era (air-cooled 1980-1983, water-cooled 1983-1986, the 1984 Wolfsburg Special Edition, and 1987-1991) but publishes no production numbers. No Volkswagen of America or NHTSA source and no period US road test (Car and Driver, Road & Track, Motor Trend or Popular Mechanics) could be retrieved for this page: the NHTSA recall query was blocked and the one road-test candidate returned an error. A US Westfalia or Syncro Westfalia count, if one exists, would have to come from Westfalia or Volkswagen of America records that were not found in this research.",
